Adjusting
---------
The Adjustments tab works on the photo in the Viewer (choosing the tab
from the Browser goes to Split View). At the top, the histogram: red, green and blue laid over each other (where they overlap
the colours add: yellow, cyan, magenta, and grey where all three do). A
small square in a top corner means shadows or highlights are clipping,
in the colour of the channels affected.

Photos and thumbnails are smoothed to the size they are shown. While a
slider is being dragged the quick half-size picture is shown as it is,
and the smooth one comes back when you let go.

The rest of the panel: Drag a slider: the
photo follows (at half resolution while you drag, then in full). Click
Adjust on a slider to put it back to its default; "Reset" beside a group
resets the group. Every change is saved as you make it.

The tools above the sliders:
  Auto          sets white balance, exposure, levels, contrast and
                vibrancy from the photo itself (one undo step; it leaves
                any crop or straightening alone)
  Crop          shows the whole photo with the crop over it. Drag a
                corner or an edge, drag inside to move it, or drag
                outside to draw a new one. Return (or Done) crops, Escape
                puts the old crop back. Photo > Adjust > Crop shape fixes the
                shape: Original, Square, 3:2, 4:3, 16:9 (it follows the
                crop's way round, so a tall crop is 2:3)
  Grid          None, Thirds, Quarters, Fine; while you straighten, a
                fine grid shows anyway
  Presets       Styles, Lumen's own looks; then your presets: apply one,
                save the current look by name, or delete one. A preset
                or style keeps the look, not the crop. Each preset is a
                Text file in <Choices$Write>.Lumen.Presets, for every
                library: copy one to another computer's Lumen.Presets,
                or drop it on Lumen's icon bar icon there, to share it
  Retouch       paints out spots (R); see Retouch and Brush below
  Brush         paints on lightening, darkening or colour (B)

Under the histogram: the photo's place, its flag, its colour label (the
row of dots: click one to give the photo that label, its own again to
take it off) and its stars.

Styles (Presets > Styles, or Selection > Apply preset > Styles for many
photos at once): Faded Film, Warm Vintage, Golden Hour, Soft Portrait,
Sunlit Garden, Vivid Landscape; Teal & Orange, Cross Process, Bleach Bypass, Nordic
Matte; Classic Black & White, High-Contrast Noir, Sepia, Cyanotype and
Platinum Tone. A style is a starting point: every slider it sets can be
changed afterwards, and Undo takes it off again.

In the Viewer, the photo shows each style as the pointer goes down the
Styles list, so you can compare them; nothing is kept until you click
one. Move off the list, or close the menu, and the photo is as it was.

Black & White: Amount takes the colour out (100: black and white).
Red, Green and Blue say how light each colour comes out, like a filter
on black and white film: lower Blue for a dark, dramatic sky, raise Red
for lighter skin and autumn leaves. They start at 30, 59 and 11.

Split Toning colours the shadows and the highlights: a hue (0 red, 30
orange, 60 yellow, 120 green, 180 cyan, 210 blue, 300 magenta) and how
strong for each. It comes after Saturation, so it tones black and white
photos too (that is how Sepia and Cyanotype are made).

Colour changes one range of colours at a time: choose one of the eight
bands (red, orange, yellow, green, aqua, blue, purple, magenta) by its
swatch, then its Hue (turns those colours towards their neighbours, up
to 30 degrees), Saturation (-100 takes the colour out, +100 doubles it)
and Luminance (a stop darker or lighter). Colours between two bands get
some of each; greys are left alone. A dot on a swatch shows a band that
has been changed. Deepening a blue sky, or quietening a green field, are
typical uses.

Noise Reduction: Colour takes out the coloured speckles of photos taken
in poor light (it smooths the colour, not the detail); Luminance smooths
the grain while keeping edges. Look at 100% (Z) to judge them; too much
Luminance makes skin and foliage look waxy.

Vignette darkens the photo towards the corners (a negative Amount) or
lightens them (positive); Midpoint is how far out from the middle it
starts. It follows any crop: the corners are the cropped photo's.

Glow lets the bright parts spill a soft light round them (an Orton
effect): their light is spread widely and laid back over the photo, so
highlights get a gentle halo while edges stay sharp. Amount is how strong;
Spread how far the light reaches; Highlights what glows (high: only the
brightest parts; low: the mid-tones too, softening the whole photo);
Warmth tints it golden (or cool, below 0).

Highlights & Shadows: Shadows brightens the dark parts of the picture and
Highlights darkens the bright parts, judged by their surroundings rather
than pixel by pixel, so detail and contrast within them stay. Good for a dark foreground under a bright sky.

Click a group's name (or its triangle) to fold it away or open it again.
The tab scrolls when it doesn't all fit.

Curves: choose RGB (all three together) or Red, Green or Blue above the
graph. Click on the curve to add a point and drag it; drag a point to
move it; Adjust-click a point to remove it. The ends can be moved too
(up from the bottom left lifts the blacks, down from the top right dims
the whites). The faint shape behind is the photo's histogram for that
channel. Up to 10 points a curve; "Reset" straightens all four.

Sharpen: Amount (0 to 200) and Radius (in pixels of the full-size
photo). It sharpens brightness only, so edges get crisper without
coloured fringes, and leaves very small differences alone so skies stay
smooth. Look at 100% (Z) to judge it; exports are sharpened at their own
size, so a small export looks as sharp as the full-size one.

White Balance: "Pick" then click something that should be grey or white
(a cloud, a wall, a shirt); "Auto" works it out from the whole photo.
Geometry: -90 and +90 turn the photo; Straighten (up to 15 degrees
either way), Vertical and Horizontal (perspective) zoom in just enough
that no empty corners show.

Retouch and Brush
-----------------
Retouch (R, or the Retouch button) paints out dust, a stray rock, a
crisp packet: click on it. Repair fills the spot from a patch close by,
chosen for you, matched to its surroundings; Clone copies the patch as
it is. The circle under the pointer is the size a spot will be: make it
a little bigger than the blemish (the Size slider, or [ and ]). Feather
(or { and }) is how much of the edge fades in. The sliders change the
spot placed or moved last too (a resized spot finds a new patch). Drag
a spot, or its grey source circle, to move it; Adjust-click takes it
away. Return, Escape or Done puts the tool away.

Brush (B, or the Brush button) paints Dodge (lighter), Burn (darker),
Saturate or Desaturate where you drag. Strength is how much (for Dodge
and Burn, up to 2 stops). One stroke over itself counts once; a second
stroke adds to the first. The sliders change the stroke painted last;
Adjust-click on a stroke's path takes it away.

Spots and strokes belong to the photo: they are not in presets, styles
or copied adjustments. They are in exports, prints and thumbnails.

Comparing
---------
O with photos selected in the Browser (or Selection > Compare) pins the
first on the left and shows the next beside it; in the Viewer, O pins
the photo shown. Left and Right change the photo on the right (the keys
rate and label it); Return pins it instead and shows the next. O or
Escape stops comparing.

