Exporting
---------
Ctrl-E (or Export... in the Selection or Photo menu) opens the Export
box. Drag its icon to a directory display, or into another program, or
type a full pathname and click Save. In the box:
  Format      JPEG (with its quality), PNG or TIFF (8 bits, LZW)
  Size        Full, the longer side at 3840, 2048 or 1024 pixels, or
              Fit in a box of so many pixels (for a screen, a web page,
              a slideshow). Photos are never made bigger
  Colour      sRGB (for screens and the web), Adobe RGB (1998) or Display
              P3 (wider colour, for printers and newer screens); the file
              carries its ICC profile
  Sharpening  for output: None, Low, Standard or High, at the size
              written (on top of the photo's own Sharpen)
  Names       for several photos: a pattern of {name} (the photo's),
              {n} or {nnn} (a counter, padded), {date} {year} {month}
              {day} {time} (taken), {project}, {camera} and {rating},
              such as {date}_{name}_{nnn}
The photo is written with all its adjustments, crop and straightening,
from the full-size original. With several photos selected, the icon is
a directory: they are written into a new directory of that name, one
file each. The box remembers its settings. Exports run in the
background; the bottom bar shows the progress.

Printing
--------
Ctrl-P (or Print... in the Selection or Photo menu) prints the selected
photos on the printer chosen in !Printers. Choose the copies, and the
size: Fit the page (all of the photo, as big as it goes), Fill the page
(to the edges; some is cut off) or a given size in centimetres (at most;
the shape is kept). Per page: 1, 2, 4, 6 or 9 photos to a page, each in
an equal space (fit and fill then mean the space), or As many as fit of
the given size, to cut out; Gap is the space between them, in mm.
"Names under the photos" prints each one's name under it (a contact
sheet). "Turn photos to suit the page" turns a landscape photo in a tall
space; "Sharpen for print" makes up for the softening of ink and paper.
The line at the bottom says how many to a page and how many pages.
Each photo is prepared at the printer's resolution in the background,
then printed in colour through the printer driver.

PostScript printers print in grey unless their Configure window in
!Printers has Colour ticked: the Print box says so when it is not.

Captions, keywords and smart albums
-----------------------------------
Ctrl-K (or Selection > Caption and keywords..., or a click on Caption or
Keywords in the Info tab) opens a window for the selected photos' caption
and keywords. Keywords are words or short phrases with commas between:
places, people, subjects. The menu button beside them lists the keywords
already in the library. For several photos at once, the window shows the
caption if they share one and the keywords they all have; the keywords
in the list go on all of them, and those taken out of it come off all of
them (keywords only some have are left as they are).

Search finds captions and keywords. Exports carry them (as XMP, which
other photo programs read), and photos tagged elsewhere bring their
keywords and caption with them when imported.

A smart album (New > Smart album..., or Album > New smart album...)
holds the photos that match what you choose: words to find (in the name,
caption, keywords, camera, project or date), the lowest rating, flagged
only, adjusted only, and the dates taken between (2026, 2026-06,
16/6/2026...). It starts from the Browser's search. It keeps up as
photos change; its menu in the Library tab changes what it looks for.
Smart albums show with a magnifying glass, and photos can't be put in
them by hand.

Versions and stacks
-------------------
A version is one way of developing a photo: Duplicate version (Ctrl-D)
makes another with the same adjustments, to try a different look (a
black and white one, a tighter crop); New version from original makes
one as it came from the camera. Both share the original file: only the
recipe is new. A version takes the caption, keywords and albums, and is
named after the file with _v2, _v3...

A stack gathers photos that belong together - a photo's versions, a
burst, a bracket - under one, the pick. A closed stack shows only its
pick, with the number in the stack on it; click the number (or press S)
to open the stack and see them all, on a grey band, the pick first.
Versions and stacks, on the Lumen menu: Stack (Ctrl-G) the selected
photos, Unstack (Ctrl-U), Make the pick (\), Open or close stack (S), and
Auto-stack, which stacks photos taken within so many seconds of each
other (the selected ones, or all those shown). Albums, Rejected and a
search show every photo in a stack.

Edited photos are marked "Edited" in the library, and their thumbnails
are made again with the edit.

Speed: !Lumen.Bench measures the editing pipeline on your machine. Run it
in a task window (Ctrl-F12):  *Run <Lumen$Dir>.Bench
With a JPEG's pathname after it (type *Run <Lumen$Dir>.Bench and a
space, then Shift-drag the photo into the task window - an ordinary drag
types the file's contents instead - or type the pathname), Bench exports
that photo at full size and says where the time goes.

Exports say how long they took in the Activity history (for a raw
file: LumenRaw developing it, its picture being read, and the adjusting
and writing), once they take a second or more.

Lumen runs on any ARMv7 processor with VFPv3. When the processor has
NEON (every Raspberry Pi from the Pi 2 on does), JPEGs are read and
written with it, and Lumen's own scaling of the photo to the screen and
its sharpening use it too; without NEON the plain code gives exactly the
same pictures. Bench says which is in use and times both. To compare:
*Set JSIMD_FORCENONE 1 (JPEGs) and *Set Lumen$NEON 0 (Lumen's own) before
running Bench or Lumen use the plain code; *Unset them to put NEON back.

*Set Lumen$JPEGEighths 1 lets Lumen decode JPEGs at any eighth of their
size (3/8 for a 6000-pixel photo shown at 1600) rather than only a half,
a quarter or an eighth. That is fewer pixels, but the NEON code covers
only the halvings, so Bench times both: use it if it's quicker for you.

The Viewer: a change shows the new picture over the old one (it is made
at the size shown before it is drawn, so nothing flickers). For a camera
raw file the full size is read in the background once the photo is on
screen, so 100% and the loupe are ready sooner; until it is, the loupe
shows the picture on screen enlarged.

