Camera raw files
----------------
Lumen reads camera raw files (Canon, Nikon, Sony, Fujifilm, Olympus,
Panasonic, Pentax, DNG and many more) with its LibRaw plug-in, which is
a separate package: LumenRaw, in PackMan or as LumenRaw-1.1.zip on
Lumen's release page (drag the LibRaw directory in its !Lumen.Plugins
into Plug-ins > Add plug-in). It goes into !Lumen.Plugins,
and Lumen finds it when it next starts (or at once, if it is added from
the Plug-ins menu). Without it, raw files are still
imported (and listed), and Lumen says what to install when you open one;
their thumbnails are made once the plug-in is there.

Thumbnails come from the camera's own preview, so they are quick. The
viewer develops the raw data with the camera's white balance and colour
in the background (a few seconds for a big file), at half size; 100%,
the loupe and exports develop it in full.

Previews: so a raw photo opens at once, Lumen keeps a preview of it in
the library (Previews, beside Thumbs): the photo developed at 1600
pixels, made in the background once the thumbnails are done, newest
first, and kept whenever the viewer develops one itself. The photo shown
in the viewer always comes first: until it is open Lumen does nothing
else. A preview is made again if the file, its orientation or LumenRaw
changes. Choices sets which photos get them (Raw files, All photos or
None) and Discard removes them; about 300-500 KB each.

Other formats
-------------
Lumen reads JPEG, PNG, TIFF and camera raw files itself. Other formats come
as plug-ins, which add them without a new Lumen: the first is LumenHEIF,
for the HEIF photos (.heic) iPhones and many other phones take, and
AVIF photos (.avif). Install
one from PackMan, or drag it into Plug-ins > Add plug-in. Each has its
own guide, in !Lumen.Plugins.<name>.Guide. Photos in a plug-in's format
are imported once it is installed: import the folder again.

Choices
-------
Choices... on the icon bar menu sets:
  Library     where the library is kept, from the next time Lumen starts
              (empty: the usual place, in Choices). Drag a directory to
              the window to fill it in. Setting Lumen$Library before
              running Lumen overrides it.
  Export      the format, size and JPEG quality the Export box starts with
  Thumbnails  the size Lumen starts with
  Previews    which photos get previews: Raw files, All photos or None;
              Discard removes them (made again unless None)
  Check the photos' files are still there when Lumen opens: switch off
              for a big library on discs that are often away.
  Folders     whether folders imported from now on are watched and get
              albums from their folders, and how often watched folders
              are looked through (in minutes)
They are kept in <Choices$Write>.Lumen.Choices.

Help
----
Lumen answers !Help: run it and point at anything in Lumen's windows or
menus. The Lumen User Guide, with pictures, is !Lumen.Guide.index/html: choose
User guide on the icon bar menu to read it in your web browser.

One Lumen at a time
-------------------
Running Lumen again while it is already running opens the one that is
running, rather than a second copy on the same library.

Double-clicking photos: File types
----------------------------------
File types, on the icon bar menu, says which photos a double-click in
the Filer opens in Lumen: Never, While Lumen is running (to begin
with), or Always, even when not running (Lumen sets those types' Run
action). Then the types: JPEG, PNG and TIFF (not ticked to begin with),
the types of your plug-ins' photos (ticked), and Data files named like
a photo (while running). A photo not in the library yet is imported,
then opened in the Viewer.
A plug-in's types come from MimeMap (InetDBase:MimeMap), which pairs
its MIME types (image/heic, image/avif...) with filetypes; RISC OS
5.31's MimeMap has none for HEIF and AVIF yet. A ticked type with no
name or icon gets MimeMap's name and Lumen's file icon, kept in
Choices:Lumen.Types and Choices:Lumen.Sprites for !Boot.

Plug-ins
--------
Plug-ins add to what Lumen can do: LumenRaw (camera raw files), and
format plug-ins for other formats. Each is a directory in
!Lumen.Plugins, where PackMan puts them.
Plug-ins, on the icon bar menu and the main window's menu, lists them:
choose one to switch it off (it stays installed, unticked) or back on.
Add plug-in... opens a window to drag a plug-in's directory into (or
drag it to Lumen's window); Lumen copies it into its Plugins. Remove
deletes one, after asking - for one PackMan installed, remove its
package in PackMan instead, so PackMan knows. Lumen remembers which are
off in its Choices.

A plug-in's directory has a text file, Plugin, saying what it is:
Name, Version, Kind, Program, Package and Description lines. A format
plug-in (Kind: format) also says which files are its own (Extensions
and Signature lines) and what one is called (Format); Lumen imports
the files it claims and runs it to read them, so a new format needs
only a new plug-in. Lumen's source has the API, docs/plugin-api.md.

Exported files
--------------
Exports keep the original's EXIF: camera, lens, exposure, date, GPS,
author and copyright (a JPEG's EXIF block, a PNG's eXIf chunk, a TIFF's tags). The
orientation, size and colour space are the export's own; the
thumbnail and the maker's notes are left out. The caption and keywords
go in as XMP (dc:description and dc:subject). For an original with no
EXIF of its own (a PNG, some raw files), what the library knows of it
is written instead.

Removing photos
---------------
Delete (or Selection > Remove from library, or the Photo menu) takes the
selected photos out of the library, after asking. Their files are never
touched: their ratings, adjustments and album places go, and they can be
imported again. Menu over a project or album in the Library tab renames
it, deletes an album (its photos stay), or removes a project and all its
photos from the library.

Libraries
---------
The icon bar menu's Open library submenu (choosing Open library itself
opens the window):
  Export library  copies the whole library to a new one: every photo,
                  version and adjustment, rating, label, caption,
                  keyword, project, album, stack and preset, with the
                  thumbnails. Drag the directory to a Filer window (or
                  type a full pathname and click Save). "Include the
                  original photos" copies the files into the new
                  library's Originals directory, a directory for each
                  project, so it can go to any disc or computer; it
                  finds them there wherever it is moved to.
                  Selection > Export as library, and Export as library
                  on a project's or album's menu, export just those.
  Import library  brings another library's photos into this one: drag
                  its directory into the window (or onto Lumen
                  anywhere). It says what would come before anything
                  changes. Photos already here aren't added twice (a
                  version with other adjustments comes in as another
                  version); projects, albums and keywords are matched
                  by name; folder albums and watching stay behind.
                  "Copy its photos into this library" copies the new
                  photos' files into this library's Originals.
  Check library   checks the catalogue file and the thumbnails for
                  anything wrong: damage, entries pointing at nothing,
                  missing or leftover thumbnails. Repair puts it right,
                  after keeping a copy of the catalogue as
                  Catalogue-backup; photos that had lost their project
                  go into one called Recovered.

Missing files
-------------
Lumen checks that each photo's file is still where it was (when it
starts, and when its window is opened again). A photo whose file has
gone - moved, renamed, or on a disc that isn't there - is marked
"Missing", and the Library tab lists them under Missing Files. If you
moved them, drag the folder they are in now into Lumen: it recognises
them by their contents, and takes their new place, with nothing added
twice.

Colour
------
Lumen shows and exports photos in sRGB, the desktop's colours. Photos in
Adobe RGB (1998) or Display P3 (iPhones') are converted as they are
read, so they look as they should rather than dull; the Info tab's
"Colour" line says what a photo is. Lumen knows them from the photo's
ICC profile or, without one, from the camera's EXIF. Other profiles are
shown as sRGB.

Needs
-----
RISC OS 5.30 or later on a Raspberry Pi 4, 400 or CM4 (other ARMv7
machines should work), and the SharedUnixLibrary 1.16+ and ARMEABISupport
modules from PackMan.

